# Concise-AI — session context

## What this is

Chrome extension (Manifest V3) named **Concise-AI**. Before send on ChatGPT/Claude, a **local rule-based** classifier decides if the prompt is a short-answer intent; if so, appends a brevity instruction to the composer text.

## Critical constraints (do not violate)

- **No API keys. No network calls. No external services.** Classifier is regex/keyword only.
- Permissions stay minimal: `storage` + hosts `https://chatgpt.com/*` and `https://claude.ai/*` only.
- No bundler / no npm runtime deps for the extension itself.
- Keep selectors in `src/site-config.js`; do not scatter DOM selectors into logic files.
- Fail silently if selectors miss — never block the user’s native send.
- `project-spec.md` and `description.md` are **gitignored** private docs; do not commit them.

## Layout

- `manifest.json` — MV3
- `src/content-script.js` — send intercept (Enter / pointer / click / submit), toggle gate
- `src/intent-classifier.js` — `classifyIntent(text) -> "short" | "normal"`
- `src/prompt-modifier.js` — `modifyPrompt(text, intent)`
- `src/site-config.js` — per-site input + send selectors
- `popup/` — enabled toggle → `chrome.storage.local` key `enabled`
- `tests/` — `node --test tests/*.test.js`

## Known v1 tradeoff

Instruction is appended into the visible sent-message bubble (DOM approach). Hiding it = network intercept = out of scope for v1.

## Git

- After meaningful edits: **commit and push** without waiting to be asked (see `git-commits.mdc`).
- Short, clear commits. **Never** add `Co-authored-by: Cursor` (strip it before push if injected).
